From seedds/nxnative (MIT), commit 04419bc2: LDG.64 / LDG.128 through the memory pool table load a uvecN once
(GlobalMemoryVector) instead of one 32-bit word at a time. The emitters mark the words of one 64- or 128-bit load
(Operation.GlobalVectorCount / GlobalVectorWord); when the address traces to no storage buffer, GlobalToStorage emits one
vector helper for all of them, which returns word 0 and leaves words 1 and up in the shader's gmem_vec local memory; the
loads of those words, which follow in the block, read them from there. Ocarina of Time's foliage mesh shaders read 464 of
their 506 raw words as 116 such loads (about 3.1 ms of a frame on an M4 Pro). The hint word (entry word 6) and the
per-entry write-log bitmap of that commit's host (nvn_mtl.m gmem_map, 1 << 13 entries) are not taken: this project's host
keeps the 1 << 12 table and the page hash set, and the vector path reads neither. The other InstEmitAmpereMem changes in
that commit (the descriptor bit, ATOMG.CAS, INC/DEC) are not part of this one.

